Subject: [PATCH v1 0/2] Add S5KC505A helper and correct software ISP
 statistics for LSC

This series adds a sensor helper for the Samsung S5KC505A and makes the
GPU software ISP's statistics account for lens shading correction.

The helper describes the sensor's linear analogue gain and black level,
allowing the software ISP to use its tunable mean-luminance AGC. The
statistics change passes the frame's LSC state and table to SwStatsCpu,
then corrects the sampled Bayer components around their black pedestal
before accumulating AWB sums and the AGC histogram. The state and consumer
are kept in one patch so each change is independently useful and builds.

The gain field follows the existing GPU shader's normalized RGBA8 scale,
bilinear filtering and texture coordinates, with gains cached in Q10.
Sparse sampling remains, and the CPU renderer retains raw statistics
because it does not apply LSC to its images.

The reported hardware platform is a Lenovo ThinkSmart View, Qualcomm
MSM8953 with qcom-camss, Samsung S5KC505A and the GPU software ISP. The
S5KC505A kernel driver used for these tests is currently out-of-tree.
Reported neutral-surface measurements improve from roughly 5% warm to
R/G = 0.999 and B/G = 0.983; an AGC target of 0.12 can replace the previous
0.10 setting, closer to the default 0.16. These measurements motivate the
change; the rebased series has not been retested on the device here.

Each patch passes the unmodified utils/checkstyle.py and a fresh AArch64
Debian trixie cross-build with the simple pipeline, softisp IPA and GPU
software ISP enabled. A QEMU helper test covers all 481 analogue gain
codes. A separate statistics test checks 300000 shader-reference gains
and 840 integration cases covering Bayer formats, phases, windows,
pedestals, histograms, saturation, caching and LSC state transitions.
The statistics test remains outside the submitted patches.

The series is based on the upstream master commit listed below.

The implementation was developed with the help of an AI coding assistant
and reviewed and tested by the author.

